   <title>Linux Landing Kernel Workaround For AMD TLBI Erratum #1718</title>
   <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/Linux-WA-TLBI-Erratum-1718</link>
   <guid>https://www.phoronix.com/news/Linux-WA-TLBI-Erratum-1718</guid>
   <description>AMD TLBI Erratum #1718 was worked around in CPU microcode for addressing a defect in certain Zen CPUs where the Translation Lookaside Buffer Invalidate Instruction use could lead to to stale translation entries. But for those on affected AMD CPUs and not running the updated microcode, a kernel worka</description>
   <pubDate>Sun, 11 Oct 2026 09:20:00 -0400</pubDate>
   </item>
  <item>
   <title>Microsoft Contributes Para-Virtualized IOMMU Hyper-V Driver For Linux</title>
   <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/MS-Paravirt-Hyper-V-IOMMU</link>
   <guid>https://www.phoronix.com/news/MS-Paravirt-Hyper-V-IOMMU</guid>
   <description>Microsoft is contributing a new kernel driver for the upcoming Linux 7.4 cycle. Queued up into the IOMMU subsystem's "-next" branch is a new para-virtualized IOMMU driver for benefiting Linux guest VMs running on Microsoft's Hyper-V virtualization platform...</description>
   <pubDate>Sun, 11 Oct 2026 08:00:00 -0400</pubDate>
   </item>
  <item>
   <title>Auto Vectorizing The RISC-V Linux Kernel Shows Promising Results</title>
   <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/Auto-Vectorize-RISC-V-Kernel</link>
   <guid>https://www.phoronix.com/news/Auto-Vectorize-RISC-V-Kernel</guid>
   <description>The Linux kernel build for RISC-V doesn't currently make use of the CPU ISA's Vector extension. but leveraging it with compiler auto vectorization is showing the ability to provide nice performance results for enhancing RISC-V Linux performance...</description>
   <pubDate>Sun, 11 Oct 2026 06:18:00 -0400</pubDate>
   </item>
  <item>
   <title>Drgn 0.3 Released For This "Natural" Programmable Debugger From Meta</title>
